<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of Jena</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1558">1558</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Friedrich Schiller University Jena</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> is a university in Jena, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, founded by the Ernestine dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and inaugurated in 1558 after they lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a>. Shared by the Ernestine duchies, it flourished around 1800, when Fichte, Schelling, Hegel and Schiller taught there and the town became a centre of German idealism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a>. It has been named the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-schiller">Friedrich Schiller</a> University since 1934.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/university-of-jena.jpg" alt="University of Jena" width="320" height="376" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Uni-Jena-logo.png: UnknownUnknown derivative work: Ba10r (talk) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uni-Jena-logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
